  <h2>What a bid proposal is (and why the exclusions matter most)</h2>
  <p>
    A construction bid proposal is the customer-facing offer: a price, the scope that price buys,
    the conditions attached to it, and a line for the customer to accept. It is the document a GC
    reads to decide whether your $486,200 and a competitor’s $471,000 are actually for the same work
    — and, months later, the document everyone re-reads when something you never priced turns out to
    be expected.
  </p>
  <p>
    That second reading is why the exclusions list is the most valuable paragraph you will write. A
    scope section says what you will do; an exclusions list says what you will not do, in words the
    other side has already accepted. Power wiring to your equipment, roof flashing, cutting and
    patching, utility fees, bonds, overtime: each one you name is an argument you never have. The
    number behind the proposal comes from the
    <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">construction estimate template</a>; this page is
    about everything wrapped around that number.
  </p>

  <h2>Section by section</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Bid documents and addenda.</strong> Drawing set and date, spec divisions, addenda acknowledged.
      If the documents change after you submit, this line is your price protection.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Scope of work.</strong> One sentence promising labor, materials, equipment and supervision,
      then bullets by system with sheet and spec references. Reference the documents; do not restate them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Base bid.</strong> One lump sum for the scope above. Say whether sales tax on materials
      is included and whether <a href="/blog/bid-bonds-vs-performance-bonds/">bonds</a> are.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Alternates and allowances.</strong> Priced options as adds and (deducts); allowances carried
      in the base bid with the note that they adjust to actual cost.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Inclusions and exclusions.</strong> Side by side so a reader sees both at once.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Schedule.</strong> Start, substantial completion, duration, and the lead times your dates
      depend on — submittal turnaround, equipment ship dates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Payment terms and conditions.</strong> Progress billing, retainage, payment due, then six
      short clauses: validity, payment, changes in writing, schedule, insurance and warranty, and what
      happens when the proposal is accepted.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Acceptance.</strong> Two signature blocks. When the second one is signed, this is an agreement.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A worked example: the $486,200 Harbor Point proposal</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example is a mechanical subcontractor’s proposal to a general contractor for the
    plumbing and <a href="/solutions/hvac-contractors/">HVAC</a> scope of a medical office building, dated
    May 4, 2026 and valid for 30 days. The base bid is $486,200.00 — the estimate total from the
    <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">estimate template</a>’s example — with sales tax on
    materials included and bonds excluded. Three alternates sit under it: high-efficiency rooftop
    units per Addendum 2 (add $28,400), a BMS graphics package (add $6,750), and deleting the
    exam-room reheat coils (deduct $9,300). One allowance is called out inside the base bid: $1,980
    to install an owner-furnished water heater, credited if deleted.
  </p>
  <p>
    That allowance is the kind of detail a proposal earns its keep on. On our
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order template</a> the same job later deletes the water
    heater installation, and the credit is exactly $1,980 — no negotiation, because the proposal already
    said what it was worth. The schedule runs June 1 to November 18, 2026, progress billing is monthly
    against a schedule of values with 10% retainage, and the GC signed the acceptance on May 18 — which
    is the contract date on every other document in the set.
  </p>

  <h2>Bid vs. proposal vs. quote vs. estimate</h2>
  <p>The words blur in practice, so here is how the four documents divide the work:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Estimate</strong> — internal cost build-up by section, with overhead and margin. Never sent
      as-is. <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">Template</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bid</strong> — the price itself, submitted into a competitive selection, often on the GC’s
      bid form.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Proposal</strong> — the bid plus scope, alternates, inclusions, exclusions, schedule and
      terms, with an acceptance line. This template.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Quote</strong> — a one-page fixed-price offer for a small direct job, with line items, a
      deposit and an acceptance signature. <a href="/templates/construction-quote/">Template</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    After acceptance the proposal is usually followed by a
    <a href="/templates/subcontractor-agreement/">subcontract</a> on the GC’s form. The agreement clause
    in this template makes the proposal the deal until that subcontract exists, and requires the subcontract
    to be consistent with it — read the subcontract against your exclusions before you sign.
  </p>

  <h2>Alternates, allowances and unit prices</h2>
  <p>
    Owners rarely have every decision made at bid time. Alternates let them decide later without
    re-bidding: price each one as a clean add or deduct against the base bid, with the same
    inclusions and exclusions, and never fold them into the lump sum. Allowances cover things that
    cannot be priced yet — a fixture package still being selected — and should be stated as carried
    amounts that adjust to actual cost with a written change order. If the bid form asks for unit
    prices, provide them as a separate schedule for adds and deducts, with a note that they apply to
    changes ordered in writing, not to correcting quantities in the documents.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes this form is built to prevent</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No validity date — a proposal from March becomes a contract price in September.</li>
    <li>Scope written as a restatement of the spec instead of a list of what you are doing.</li>
    <li>Exclusions left generic ("anything not listed") — courts and GCs read that as nothing.</li>
    <li>
      Alternates buried in the lump sum, making your base bid look high against a stripped one.
    </li>
    <li>No schedule assumptions, so a late submittal approval becomes your delay.</li>
    <li>Payment terms missing retainage, so the subcontract sets it at whatever the GC wants.</li>
    <li>Sending the estimate instead of the proposal and inviting line-item negotiation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The full process from plans to submission is in our guide to
    <a href="/blog/how-to-create-construction-bid/">how to create a construction bid</a>. Pair this
    proposal with the <a href="/templates/scope-of-work/">scope of work template</a> when the scope needs
    more room than a page, and with the
    <a href="/templates/subcontractor-agreement/">subcontractor agreement</a> for what comes next.
  </p>
